WEBVTT

00:00:00.156 --> 00:00:04.212
Now we deliberately challenge the database rules.

00:00:04.212 --> 00:00:06.132
The examples test duplicate natural keys, negative prices, orphan foreign-key references,

00:00:06.132 --> 00:00:12.232
and impossible time ranges. These are useful tests because a good design should reject them.

00:00:12.232 --> 00:00:14.782
Run the provided invalid example and read the PostgreSQL error.

00:00:14.782 --> 00:00:17.682
Do not treat the rejection as a failed lesson.

00:00:17.682 --> 00:00:20.922
Then connect the error to the constraint responsible for it.

00:00:20.922 --> 00:00:22.942
A strong schema is not only one that accepts valid data.

00:00:22.942 --> 00:00:26.362
It is also one that reliably refuses invalid data.

00:00:26.362 --> 00:00:31.682
When designing your own tables, plan at least one invalid-data test for each important rule.

00:00:31.682 --> 00:00:34.912
That helps confirm the guardrail actually works.

00:00:34.912 --> 00:00:37.852
Connect this idea to a clinic system separating patients, encounters, clinicians, and billing.

00:00:37.852 --> 00:00:43.762
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:43.762 --> 00:00:48.747
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:48.747 --> 00:00:51.647
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:51.647 --> 00:00:57.040
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
